checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3208:## Critical and Major Findings
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3210:1. **Major, grade B: the payroll estimate needs its uncertainty beside the headline.** Page bytes: “payroll employment rose by 29,000.” The [pinned BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:454) gives “about 122,000” as the threshold for a statistically significant monthly establishment-survey change; its technical note labels the newest estimates preliminary. The correct account is a **+29,000 point estimate**, below that threshold, alongside a meaningful 60,000 downward revision to July and August. Put that distinction in the story view so readers do not treat the sign of one month’s estimate as settled.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3212:2. **Major, grade B: the rounded unemployment rise has countervailing evidence.** Page bytes: “unemployment ticks up.” In the [BLS household summary](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:328), the exact rows show labor force change “485,” employed change “406,” and unemployed change “78,” all in thousands. [St. Louis Fed calculations](https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2026/oct/flash-report-unemployment-rises-slightly-job-growth-slows-september) put the unrounded rate at “4.1413%” in August and “4.1754%” in September. The correct account is a small increase in unemployment **while employment and participation also rose**. This changes the easiest wrong reading of the headline. Those household figures appear in Just the facts, but the default story hides them.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3214:3. **Major, grade B: the national rate conceals a substantial group difference.** Page bytes: “unemployment rate was 4.2 percent.” The [BLS summary row](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:348) reads “Black or African American | 7.6 | 6.3 | 6.0 | 7.0 | 1.0.” The correct account is that Black unemployment rose **one percentage point from August to September**, even as the national rounded rate moved one tenth. The story view should carry that distributional change. The same row shows 7.0 percent remained below September 2025’s 7.6 percent, so this is a monthly finding, not a claim of yearlong deterioration.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3216:4. **Major, grade B: “hiring” is the wrong measured quantity.** Page bytes: “shows slowdown in hiring.” The September BLS release measures the **net change in payroll employment**. The latest available [BLS JOLTS record](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/jolts_09292026.pdf) says August “Hires changed little at 5.2 million” and schedules September gross-hiring data for November 3. The correct account is that **net payroll growth slowed from August’s revised gain**; September gross hiring had not yet been reported. Use “payroll growth” in the headline, and distinguish hiring and separations when discussing the labor market’s movement.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3218:5. **Major, grade B: the page gives no independent countercheck or adequate baseline for its slowdown reading.** Page bytes: “+29,000” and “Where the month sits.” The [BLS release’s private-sector row](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:393) ends “| 46 |,” meaning **+46,000 private payroll jobs**; government’s row ends “| -17 |.” [ADP’s separate payroll record](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September) says “Private employers added 90,000 jobs in September.” These are different surveys, not directly interchangeable results. The [pinned PAYEMS series](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/PAYEMS-2026-10-03.csv) also yields about **9,700 jobs per month in 2025** against **68,000 in January through September 2026**. The correct account is a weak September within a year stronger than 2025, with a separate measure showing stronger September private growth. The threshold for payroll growth that holds unemployment steady is itself disputed: [Dallas Fed’s estimate](https://www.dallasfed.org/research/economics/2026/0331) approached zero in late 2025, but it is not a verified September 2026 threshold.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3220:6. **Major, grade B: the consequence and next decision are outside the chronology.** Page bytes: “Next Employment Situation, for October.” The [Federal Reserve’s September 16 statement](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916a.htm) says it raised its policy-rate range by “1/4 percentage point”; its [calendar](https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm) places the next meeting on October 27–28. The [BEA’s August record](https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-august-2026) gives 3.4 percent annual PCE inflation. The correct account is that this jobs release arrives between a rate hike and the next policy decision, while elevated inflation complicates the interpretation. The page should explain that stake without predicting the Fed’s decision.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3222:7. **Major: the multiple-jobholder figure is off by a factor of 1,000.** Page bytes: “counted 8,986 people working more than one job.” The [pinned Table A-9](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09.txt:219) states “[Numbers in thousands],” and its [multiple-jobholder row](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09.txt:274) ends “| 8,805 | 8,986 |.” The correct account is **8.986 million people in September**, up **181,000** from August. The page’s next sentence acknowledges the table’s units but does not fix its stated count.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3238:| **Minor, grade C:** diffusion-index definition | “the share of 250 private industries adding jobs” | The [BLS footnote](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:440) includes “plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment.” The index is **49.0 under that formula**, not simply the share adding jobs. This is in Just the facts and does not change a seven-answer conclusion. |
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.log:3241:**Passage grades, grouped by the answer they can change:** A: BLS headline and July/August revisions, carried in the story. B: survey uncertainty, household balance, Black unemployment, private/government split, historical baseline, gross-hiring distinction, and policy context; these are hidden or absent as detailed above. C: U-6, prime-age participation, longer-term unemployment, wage arithmetic, forecasts, and detailed industry rows; where supplied, Just the facts or Show the work is sufficient. D: release logistics and repetitive coverage that change none of the seven answers. Table A-9’s multiple-jobholder passage would ordinarily be C, but its **incorrect unit** makes the accuracy error Major.
